      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "The Investment",
        "title": "Studio costs add up. The FLEX pays for itself fast.",
        "text": "At $1,200 AUD, the FLEX delivers a compelling return on investment. For a dual-discipline user who previously maintained both a Pilates studio membership ($280/month) and a gym membership ($25/week), the combined savings of $388/month mean the FLEX pays for itself in just over 3 months. After that, you're saving nearly $4,700 per year."
      }
    },
    "sp_reviews": {
      "type": "sensol-reviews",
      "blocks": {
        "review_1": {
          "type": "review",
          "settings": {
            "text": "I've been doing reformer Pilates at studios for three years and honestly didn't expect a home machine to match the experience. The FLEX proved me wrong. The servo motor resistance is smoother than any spring system I've used. The Off-centre training mode is something you simply can't replicate on a traditional reformer, and it's been a game-changer for my core stability. I cancelled my studio membership and I'm actually training more consistently now.",
            "name": "Emma R.",
            "role": "Perth"
          }
        },
        "review_2": {
          "type": "review",
          "settings": {
            "text": "The dual-function capability is what sold me, and it's even better than I expected. I do Pilates three days a week and strength training two days — all on the same machine. The barbell bar and short handles are surprisingly versatile. The 90 kg max resistance is plenty for everything except heavy powerlifting. I've cancelled both my Pilates studio membership ($280/month) and my gym membership ($25/week). The FLEX paid for itself in under three months.",
            "name": "Marcus T.",
            "role": "Gold Coast"
          }
        },
        "review_3": {
          "type": "review",
          "settings": {
            "text": "As a physiotherapist, I'm cautious about recommending home fitness equipment. The FLEX is different — the 0.5 kg increments allow me to prescribe exact resistance levels for my patients' home programmes, and the three training modes offer rehabilitation protocols I can't replicate with spring-based reformers. The Constant Mode is particularly valuable for post-surgical rehabilitation, where maintaining even tension throughout the range of motion is critical. I've started recommending the FLEX to patients who need ongoing reformer work.",
            "name": "Dr. Sarah L.",
            "role": "Physiotherapist, Brisbane"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "review_1",
        "review_2",
        "review_3"
      ],
